  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 AM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nToday into tonight...Warm and breezy day on tap today with \nhighs peaking in the mid to upper 80s. A tightening pressure \ngradient will bring breezy winds out of the south, with gusts \n30-35mph possible in Western Minnesota. As the front passes \novernight, winds will flip out of north / northwest. A vigorous\nshortwave will drive a low pressure system over central Canada\nalong with a trailing cold front that will bring a round of \nmuch needed precipitation to northwestern Minnesota. However, \nprospects of more than a quarter inch of rain are low across our\nforecast area. Guidance hints at some attempts at mid-afternoon \nelevated showers north and west of the Twin Cities, though \ncapping and ample dry air in the lower levels will limit \ncoverage (and much in the way of precipitation reaching the \nsurface). Our main round of precip will arrive late Monday night\ninto Tuesday morning. The SPC has issued a Day 1 Slight Risk \nfor much of northwest and north central Minnesota, with the \neastern extent of the Slight Risk covering the northwestern \nhalf of Stevens, Douglas, & Todd counties. The most intense \nconvection will occur across the Red River Valley and is\nforecast to weaken as it moves east overnight. Still, there may\nbe a few instances of damaging wind or hail across western \nMinnesota.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday...Recent guidance suggests that convection \nreinvigorates near the MN/IA border during the day on Tuesday, \nalthough confidence is higher for precip on the Iowa side. Have \namended PoPs near the border to reflect this change. We've also \nintroduced a mention of patchy smoke into the grids on Tuesday \nfollowing the passage of the front. However, confidence is low on \nair quality effects and trends will need to be monitored. We'll get \na short break from the heat Tuesday into Wednesday as highs peak in \nthe mid 70s, nearing 80 in the Twin Cities. Dews will fall into the \n50s, making for a comfortable couple days!\n\nThursday onward...The heat we know and love makes a comeback \nThursday as highs return to the mid 80s, climbing to the upper 80s \nSaturday and Sunday. Upper level flow becomes increasingly zonal as \nwe approach the weekend. Precip chances may also return over the \nweekend, although forcing remains nebulous at this time.",
